WEBVTT

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:29.520
Good morning to you today, Mike.

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:34.880
Hey Shannon, good morning to y'all down in Houston and across the planet.

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:37.720
Just wanted to say thanks for the song.

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:43.430
That was by the band Rush, and my friends Ken Fisher and Greg Schertz sent that up for me.

00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:49.120
And Rush was really inspired by the launch of STS-1, so they included that in their music,

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:53.050
and it was really inspirational for them, for the whole, for the album.

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:59.740
What's really cool about it is that Superb is really inspired by our planet.

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:01.100
for such a long time.

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:04.140
So this song was a tribute to the space shuttle program

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:05.760
and so we'd like to say good morning.

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:21.040
We've got them together and we're going to start with the PDRS.

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:27.980
Shuttle pilot Greg Johnson calling down

00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:29.820
that the crew is ready to get started.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:36.600
with the docked late inspection ready to perform its last major task

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:42.140
as a member of the Space Shuttle STS-134 mission.

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:46.300
It will be delivered over to the International Space Station

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:51.860
and become a permanent component of the ISS during the fourth of the spacewalks,

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:54.920
the fourth and final spacewalk planned during Endeavour's mission.

00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:59.580
This is Gerhard Daum with the German Space Agency and Space Expo Association.

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:05.900
question for Box. You are the prime operator of the SMRMS. What was your most challenging

00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:10.660
robotics task and what was it like working at the robotics station in the cupola with

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:21.340
that view to Earth? Well, Gerhard, I'll tell you what. The cupola was everything that I

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:29.980
had heard from other people times 10. It's a magnificent view. You have a view

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.060
of literally half of the space station operating from the cupola.

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:40.550
Some of the robotic ops that we did on EVA-2 didn't even need any cameras.

00:02:40.570 --> 00:02:41.830
You could just look out the window.

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:50.640
A lot of the robotic ops that we're doing on this mission are located on the other side of the station.

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:53.920
For example, when we installed AMS and ELC-3.

00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:59.680
So the advantage of the cupola wasn't apparent as far as looking out the window.

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:07.920
But just as a place to be, as a place to operate the robotic arm, it was a wonderful, and it is a wonderful, wonderful place to be.

00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:16.210
Mike Fink, on Friday, you guys are going to go over 1,000 hours in EVA time during assembly of ISS for more than 40 days,

00:03:16.330 --> 00:03:21.010
which is either an incredible milestone or maybe it's just a trivial milestone.

00:03:21.010 --> 00:03:21.390
I don't know.

00:03:21.430 --> 00:03:24.090
How do you look at that, 1,000 hours of EVA time?

00:03:24.250 --> 00:03:24.470
Thanks.

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:29.970
Yeah, 1,000 hours, that's...

00:03:30.020 --> 00:03:40.320
That's pretty impressive. In fact, we had concerns when we were first designing the space station of how much time, EVA, zero-G EVA time it would take.

00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:44.540
At that point, we weren't really as experienced with EVA.

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:55.870
The shuttle program paved the way with some of the earlier EVAs, getting our extravehicular mobility unit, EMU, up and running, and then getting it to really shine.

00:03:55.870 --> 00:03:59.870
and some of the tools and techniques that came along for space station building.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:05.340
are very helpful. The stints that we did at Hubble Space Telescope, we still use some

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:09.980
of those tools to this day for the space station. So we've really come a long way with space

00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:13.400
walking. We've learned a lot from our Russian partners and they've learned a lot from us.

00:04:13.660 --> 00:04:19.980
And the new suits that we have and their capabilities allow us to do longer space walks.

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:25.560
I don't think anybody 10, 20, 30 years ago would imagine that we would have so many eight-hour

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:29.840
I wanted to.

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:37.700
ask Mark Kelly about the upcoming atmospheric reentry and landing. This is going to be a

00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:42.440
night landing, and I think it might be your first night landing, and I'm wondering...

00:04:42.570 --> 00:04:46.490
What do you think the challenges are going to be?

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:51.560
If you could kind of give us an idea of what do you think it'll be like.

00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:51.900
Thanks.

00:04:55.750 --> 00:04:58.190
Well, the biggest challenge is it's going to be dark.

00:04:58.980 --> 00:04:59.820
You know, that's...

00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:08.360
You know, night landings, the fact that you don't have a horizon out the window could be a little bit more of a challenge,

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:10.580
but these are mostly instrument approaches.

00:05:10.920 --> 00:05:11.760
I mean, they really are.

00:05:11.860 --> 00:05:14.970
I mean, we fly the equivalent of what an ILA.

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:21.200
would be in an airplane. It's a much steeper glide slope, about 20 degrees instead of about 2 or 3 degrees.

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:29.980
So, but we fly the approach exactly the same. We have some very bright lights on the runway, so once you get down...

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:35.060
below about a hundred or fifty feet it's almost like daytime there when you get

00:05:35.060 --> 00:05:36.520
get very close to the ground

00:05:37.210 --> 00:05:42.750
I've got a lot of night landings, over I think about 100 on an aircraft carrier.

00:05:42.910 --> 00:05:45.950
Those are challenging too, so I've kind of been in this position before.

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:47.880
I'm ready for it.

00:05:48.170 --> 00:05:49.370
and look forward to it.
